Subject: virtual-net: answer a listener's readiness without accepting

A host listener answered whether a connection was waiting by accepting
one and keeping it in a backlog of its own, and an accept then took it
from there. That is a second place readiness can live, which anything
that looks only at the kernel descriptor - a poll, an epoll set, another
thread - then has to be told about. Leaving the connection in the kernel
is simpler.

Readiness is now one zero-timeout poll on the listening descriptor,
which reports a pending connection without taking it, and an accept
takes it from the kernel when the guest asks. The same probe already
decided whether to reassert readiness after an accept; on Windows,
where that was a stub, it is WSAPoll, so the reassert works there too.
Windows also needs the interest put back when a probe finds nothing,
whether asked for readiness or after an accept that emptied the queue:
mio's readiness there is one-shot, re-armed by an operation returning
WouldBlock, which the accept in the old readiness check was and a probe
of the kernel is not. Selector::rearm registers the source again, as
mio itself does on WouldBlock.

